<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Review problems on partial derivatives
-->
<worksheet xml:id="worksheet-exercises-partial-derivatives" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Exercises for <xref ref="chapter-partial-derivatives" text="title"/></title>

<introduction>
<p>
These problems compute higher order partial derivatives. If you would like to review this topic before
attempting them, see <xref ref="chapter-partial-derivatives" text="title"/>.
</p>
</introduction>

<exercise xml:id="rm2-pd-1">
<title>Higher order computation</title>
<statement>
<p>
Prove that <m>c(x,t) = \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{Dt}}\,e^{-\frac{x^2}{4Dt}}</m>
is a solution of the diffusion equation
<md>
\frac{\partial c}{\partial t} = D\,\frac{\partial^2 c}{\partial x^2}.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We compute the needed derivatives:
<md>
<mrow>\frac{\partial c}{\partial t}
\amp = -\frac{1}{2\sqrt{D}\,t^{3/2}}\,e^{-x^2/4Dt}
+ \frac{1}{\sqrt{Dt}}\,\frac{x^2}{4Dt^2}\,e^{-x^2/4Dt}</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{\partial c}{\partial x}
\amp = -\frac{x}{2Dt}\,\frac{1}{\sqrt{Dt}}\,e^{-x^2/4Dt}</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{\partial^2 c}{\partial x^2}
\amp = -\frac{1}{2Dt\sqrt{Dt}}\,e^{-x^2/4Dt}
+ \frac{x^2}{4D^2t^2}\,\frac{1}{\sqrt{Dt}}\,e^{-x^2/4Dt}</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
We see that the right hand side of the third equation is
<m>\dfrac{1}{D}</m> times the right hand side of the first, i.e.
multiplying <m>\dfrac{\partial^2 c}{\partial x^2}</m> by <m>D</m>
gives exactly <m>\dfrac{\partial c}{\partial t}</m>, showing that
<m>c</m> satisfies the diffusion equation.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-28">
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>f(x,y,z) = xy + 3y^2z^3 + \ln(xz)</m>. Compute
<m>\dfrac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x^2}</m> and
<m>\dfrac{\partial^2 f}{\partial y \partial z}</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Note first that <m>\ln(xz) = \ln x + \ln z</m>, which makes the
differentiation easier. Treating the other variables as constants,
<md>
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x} = y + \frac1x
\;\Rightarrow\;
\frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x^2} = -\frac{1}{x^2}.
</md>
For the mixed partial, differentiate first with respect to <m>z</m>
and then with respect to <m>y</m>:
<md>
\frac{\partial f}{\partial z} = 9y^2z^2 + \frac1z
\;\Rightarrow\;
\frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial y \partial z}
= \frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(9y^2z^2 + \frac1z\right)
= 18yz^2.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="q1-3">
<statement>
<p>
A metal plate occupies the square <m>0 \le x \le 5</m>,
<m>0 \le y \le 5</m>, and is heated so that the temperature at the
point <m>(x,y)</m> is <m>T(x,y)</m>, measured in degrees Fahrenheit.
Distances are measured in feet. The table below records some measured
values of <m>T</m>.
</p>

<table xml:id="tab-q1-temps">
<title>Measured temperatures <m>T(x,y)</m> in <m>{}^{\circ}\text{F}</m></title>
<tabular halign="center" top="medium" left="medium">
<row bottom="medium">
<cell></cell><cell right="medium"><m>y=1</m></cell><cell><m>y=1.5</m></cell><cell><m>y=2</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>x=1</m></cell><cell right="medium"><m>70</m></cell><cell><m>65</m></cell><cell><m>58</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>x=1.5</m></cell><cell right="medium"><m>74</m></cell><cell><m>68</m></cell><cell><m>59</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>x=2</m></cell><cell right="medium"><m>79</m></cell><cell><m>75</m></cell><cell><m>65</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</table>

<p>
<ol marker="(a)">
<li>
<p>
Approximate <m>T_x(1,1)</m> and interpret your answer, with units, in
the context of the problem.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Approximate <m>T_{xy}(1,1)</m>, and give its units.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
An ant walks across the plate along the path
<md>
x = 1 - \frac{\ln(2t-3)}{8}, \qquad y = \frac{2^t}{4},
\qquad 1.5 \lt t \lt 4 .
</md>
Approximate the rate at which the temperature the ant feels is
changing at the moment it is at the point <m>(x,y) = (1,1)</m>, and
give the units.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<term>(a)</term> A partial derivative is a limit of difference
quotients, so with the data we have we approximate it by a difference
quotient. Holding <m>y=1</m> fixed and stepping forward in <m>x</m>
from <m>x=1</m> to <m>x=1.5</m>,
<md>
T_x(1,1) \approx \frac{T(1.5,1) - T(1,1)}{1.5-1}
         = \frac{74-70}{0.5}
         = 8 .
</md>
The units are degrees Fahrenheit per foot. The interpretation: at the
point <m>(1,1)</m>, if the ant moves in the positive <m>x</m>-direction
while its <m>y</m>-coordinate stays fixed, the temperature increases at
a rate of about <m>8\,{}^{\circ}\text{F}</m> per foot.
</p>

<p>
<term>(b)</term> By definition <m>T_{xy} = (T_x)_y</m>, so we compute
<m>T_x</m> at two different values of <m>y</m> and then take a
difference quotient in <m>y</m>. We already have
<m>T_x(1,1) \approx 8</m>, and in the same way
<md>
T_x(1,1.5) \approx \frac{T(1.5,1.5) - T(1,1.5)}{0.5}
           = \frac{68-65}{0.5} = 6 .
</md>
Therefore
<md>
T_{xy}(1,1) \approx \frac{T_x(1,1.5) - T_x(1,1)}{1.5-1}
            = \frac{6-8}{0.5} = -4 .
</md>
The units are degrees Fahrenheit per foot per foot, i.e.
<m>{}^{\circ}\text{F}/\text{ft}^2</m>. This says that as we move in the
<m>y</m>-direction, the rate at which the temperature rises in the
<m>x</m>-direction is itself decreasing.
</p>

<p>
<term>(c)</term> First we find the value of <m>t</m> at which the ant
is at <m>(1,1)</m>. We need
<md>
<mrow>1 - \frac{\ln(2t-3)}{8} = 1
\amp \quad\Longrightarrow\quad \ln(2t-3) = 0</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad\Longrightarrow\quad 2t-3 = 1
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad t = 2 ,</mrow>
</md>
and indeed <m>y = 2^2/4 = 1</m> at <m>t=2</m>, as required. Note that
<m>t=2</m> lies in the given interval <m>1.5 \lt t \lt 4</m>.
</p>

<p>
Next we differentiate the path:
<md>
<mrow>\frac{dx}{dt} \amp = -\frac{1}{8}\cdot\frac{2}{2t-3}
              = -\frac{1}{4(2t-3)},
\amp \frac{dx}{dt}\bigg|_{t=2} \amp = -\frac{1}{4} ,</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{dy}{dt} \amp = \frac{2^t \ln 2}{4},
\amp \frac{dy}{dt}\bigg|_{t=2} \amp = \frac{4\ln 2}{4} = \ln 2 .</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
We also need <m>T_y(1,1)</m>, which we approximate from the table just
as before, stepping forward in <m>y</m>:
<md>
T_y(1,1) \approx \frac{T(1,1.5) - T(1,1)}{0.5} = \frac{65-70}{0.5}
         = -10 .
</md>
</p>

<p>
Now the chain rule gives
<md>
<mrow>\frac{dT}{dt} \amp = T_x\,\frac{dx}{dt} + T_y\,\frac{dy}{dt}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \approx (8)\left(-\frac14\right) + (-10)(\ln 2)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = -2 - 10\ln 2 \approx -8.93 .</mrow>
</md>
The units are degrees Fahrenheit per unit of <m>t</m> (per minute, if
<m>t</m> is measured in minutes). So the ant is cooling off at about
<m>8.9\,{}^{\circ}\text{F}</m> per unit of time. This makes sense: the
ant is moving slightly in the <m>-x</m> direction, which cools it a
little, and it is moving in the <m>+y</m> direction, which cools it a
lot. <xref ref="fig-q1-ant"/> shows the situation.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-q1-ant">
<caption>
The nine data points of <xref ref="tab-q1-temps"/> and the ant's path.
At <m>t = 2</m> the ant is at <m>(1,1)</m> and its velocity is
<m>\left\langle -\tfrac14,\ \ln 2\right\rangle</m>. Temperatures fall
as we move up and to the left, so the ant is cooling.
</caption>
<image width="80%">
<description>
<p>
A grid of nine labelled temperature readings on the x y plane, together
with the ant's curved path passing through the point one comma one, and
the velocity vector of the ant drawn at that point pointing up and
slightly to the left.
</p>
</description>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-q1-ant">
<diagram dimensions="(430,430)" margins="34">
<definition>ant(t) = (1 - log(2*t-3)/8, 2^t/4)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(0.5,0.5,2.5,2.5)">
<grid-axes at="q1a-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<parametric-curve at="q1a-path" function="ant" domain="(1.55,2.85)"
                  stroke="#D55E00" thickness="2.5"/>
<vector at="q1a-vel" v="(-0.25,0.69)" tail="(1,1)"
        stroke="#8B1A4A" thickness="2"/>
<point at="q1a-p11" p="(1,1)" fill="#8B1A4A"/>
<point at="q1a-a" p="(1,1.5)" fill="#0072B2"/>
<point at="q1a-b" p="(1,2)" fill="#0072B2"/>
<point at="q1a-c" p="(1.5,1)" fill="#0072B2"/>
<point at="q1a-d" p="(1.5,1.5)" fill="#0072B2"/>
<point at="q1a-e" p="(1.5,2)" fill="#0072B2"/>
<point at="q1a-f" p="(2,1)" fill="#0072B2"/>
<point at="q1a-g" p="(2,1.5)" fill="#0072B2"/>
<point at="q1a-h" p="(2,2)" fill="#0072B2"/>
<label at="q1a-l11" anchor="(0.97,1.0)" alignment="w" color="#8B1A4A">
<m>70</m>
</label>
<label at="q1a-la" anchor="(0.97,1.5)" alignment="w" color="#0072B2"><m>65</m></label>
<label at="q1a-lb" anchor="(0.97,2)" alignment="w" color="#0072B2"><m>58</m></label>
<label at="q1a-lc" anchor="(1.5,1)" alignment="sw" color="#0072B2"><m>74</m></label>
<label at="q1a-ld" anchor="(1.5,1.5)" alignment="sw" color="#0072B2"><m>68</m></label>
<label at="q1a-le" anchor="(1.5,2)" alignment="sw" color="#0072B2"><m>59</m></label>
<label at="q1a-lf" anchor="(2,1)" alignment="sw" color="#0072B2"><m>79</m></label>
<label at="q1a-lg" anchor="(2,1.5)" alignment="sw" color="#0072B2"><m>75</m></label>
<label at="q1a-lh" anchor="(2,2)" alignment="sw" color="#0072B2"><m>65</m></label>
<label at="q1a-vlab" anchor="(0.78,1.74)" alignment="ne" color="#8B1A4A">
<m>\left\langle x'(2), y'(2)\right\rangle</m>
</label>
<label at="q1a-plab" anchor="(1.55,2.30)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
<m>\text{ant's path}</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A portion of the x y plane between x equals zero point five five and two point four five. Nine data points are marked at the corners of a grid with x and y each equal to one, one point five and two, each labelled with its temperature. An orange curve, the ant's path, rises steeply through the point one comma one, and a dark red velocity arrow at that point points up and slightly to the left.">
  <annotation ref="q1a-path" text="The ant's path, given parametrically by x equals one minus one eighth of the natural log of two t minus three, and y equals two to the t over four."/>
  <annotation ref="q1a-vel" text="The velocity vector of the ant at t equals two, with components negative one quarter and natural log of two."/>
  <annotation ref="q1a-p11" text="The point one comma one, where the temperature is seventy degrees."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="q2-3">
<statement>
<p>
A metal plate occupies the square <m>0 \le x \le 5</m>,
<m>0 \le y \le 5</m>, and is heated so that the temperature at the
point <m>(x,y)</m> is <m>T(x,y)</m>, measured in degrees Fahrenheit.
Distances are measured in feet. The table below records some measured
values of <m>T</m>.
</p>

<table xml:id="tab-q2-temps">
<title>Measured temperatures <m>T(x,y)</m> in <m>{}^{\circ}\text{F}</m></title>
<tabular halign="center" top="medium" left="medium">
<row bottom="medium">
<cell></cell><cell right="medium"><m>y=1</m></cell><cell><m>y=1.5</m></cell><cell><m>y=2</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>x=1</m></cell><cell right="medium"><m>70</m></cell><cell><m>73</m></cell><cell><m>79</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>x=1.5</m></cell><cell right="medium"><m>65</m></cell><cell><m>69</m></cell><cell><m>75</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>x=2</m></cell><cell right="medium"><m>58</m></cell><cell><m>60</m></cell><cell><m>65</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</table>

<p>
<ol marker="(a)">
<li>
<p>
Approximate <m>T_x(1,1)</m> and interpret your answer, with units, in
the context of the problem.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Approximate <m>T_{xy}(1,1)</m>, and give its units.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
An ant walks across the plate along the path
<md>
x = 1 - \frac{\ln(2t-3)}{4}, \qquad y = \frac{2^t}{4},
\qquad 1.5 \lt t \lt 4 .
</md>
Approximate the rate at which the temperature the ant feels is
changing at the moment it is at the point <m>(x,y) = (1,1)</m>, and
give the units.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<term>(a)</term> Holding <m>y=1</m> and stepping forward in <m>x</m>,
<md>
T_x(1,1) \approx \frac{T(1.5,1) - T(1,1)}{0.5} = \frac{65-70}{0.5}
         = -10 \quad {}^{\circ}\text{F}/\text{ft} .
</md>
So at <m>(1,1)</m>, moving in the positive <m>x</m>-direction with
<m>y</m> held fixed, the temperature <em>drops</em> at a rate of about
<m>10\,{}^{\circ}\text{F}</m> per foot.
</p>

<p>
<term>(b)</term> We need <m>T_x</m> at a second value of <m>y</m>:
<md>
T_x(1,1.5) \approx \frac{T(1.5,1.5) - T(1,1.5)}{0.5}
           = \frac{69-73}{0.5} = -8 .
</md>
Then
<md>
T_{xy}(1,1) \approx \frac{T_x(1,1.5) - T_x(1,1)}{0.5}
            = \frac{-8-(-10)}{0.5} = 4
            \quad {}^{\circ}\text{F}/\text{ft}^2 .
</md>
</p>

<p>
<term>(c)</term> As in <xref ref="q1-3"/>, the ant is at
<m>(1,1)</m> when <m>\ln(2t-3) = 0</m>, that is at <m>t = 2</m>, and
then <m>y = 2^2/4 = 1</m> as required. Differentiating the path,
<md>
<mrow>\frac{dx}{dt} \amp = -\frac{1}{4}\cdot\frac{2}{2t-3}
              = -\frac{1}{2(2t-3)},
\amp \frac{dx}{dt}\bigg|_{t=2} \amp = -\frac{1}{2} ,</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{dy}{dt} \amp = \frac{2^t\ln 2}{4},
\amp \frac{dy}{dt}\bigg|_{t=2} \amp = \ln 2 .</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
From the table,
<md>
T_y(1,1) \approx \frac{T(1,1.5)-T(1,1)}{0.5} = \frac{73-70}{0.5} = 6 .
</md>
</p>

<p>
By the chain rule,
<md>
<mrow>\frac{dT}{dt} \amp = T_x\,\frac{dx}{dt} + T_y\,\frac{dy}{dt}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \approx (-10)\left(-\frac12\right) + (6)(\ln 2)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 5 + 6\ln 2 \approx 9.16 ,</mrow>
</md>
in degrees Fahrenheit per unit of <m>t</m>. Here the ant is
<em>warming up</em>: it moves in the <m>-x</m> direction, where the
plate is hotter, and in the <m>+y</m> direction, where the plate is
also hotter, so both contributions are positive. Compare this with
<xref ref="q1-3"/>, where the same computation gave a negative rate.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

</worksheet>
